# Line-ending pin for shell hooks. NOT a style preference — a correctness fix.
#
# This repository has core.autocrlf=true (measured 2026-08-11) and, until this
# file, no .gitattributes at all. Under that setting git rewrites LF to CRLF on
# CHECKOUT. A /bin/sh script checked out with CRLF has a shebang of
# "#!/bin/sh\r", which the interpreter cannot resolve — the hook then fails to
# execute rather than refusing anything.
#
# That failure mode is silent and it is the dangerous one: git skips a hook it
# cannot run without reporting anything, so .githooks/commit-msg would appear
# installed while enforcing nothing. An inert guard is worse than an absent one,
# because it stops anyone looking.
#
# Scoped deliberately to .githooks only. The engine repo
# (Desktop/toolsenabled-current) solves this with a repo-wide `* -text`, which it
# needs for FRA byte-fidelity hashing. This repo has no such requirement, and a
# repo-wide rule here would change the checkout bytes of every file for all
# worktrees sharing this repository — a far larger change than the problem.
/.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Font binaries must never be line-ending normalised: autocrlf rewriting a
# CRLF byte pair inside a woff2 stream corrupts it silently (the website lane
# hit exactly this shipping the same files).
*.woff2 binary

# SOURCE IS LF ON EVERY CHECKOUT. Measured 2026-09-02 on a fresh clone under
# the machine-wide core.autocrlf=true this file already documents: five suites
# that read shell/main.cjs, shell/agent-host.cjs and src/agent-session.js as
# text and search for a column-zero closing brace failed on PRISTINE sources,
# because checkout had rewritten them to CRLF. The sealed cut runs those suites
# inside a worktree made the same way, so a cut from such a clone stops at the
# test ratchet. Pinning the source extensions removes the dependence on the
# cutting machine's global git configuration.
#
# WIDENED THE SAME DAY to every text file. The suites also read the design
# docs, the stylesheets and JSON as text (fleet-trees-multi looks for a
# "```bounds" fence followed by a bare LF; home-feed-dense-tokens and
# rail-chrome match a selector, a comma and a bare LF in the CSS; preview-honesty compares prose
# lines). Eight of them failed on a pristine worktree for the same reason the
# five above did. Every blob in this repository is already LF (git ls-files
# --eol reports no i/crlf), so this rule changes checkout bytes only, and it
# makes them match what the tests and the packager expect on every machine.
# The .githooks and woff2 rules above still stand on their own.
* text=auto eol=lf
